# Biomedical image review workspace: research proposal

**Stage:** independent brief-only research and proposal. **Evidence cutoff:** 2026-10-05. **Product boundary:** research image review and annotation, not diagnosis. The scope is a first implementable increment for six researchers exchanging closed project bundles on ordinary shared storage. This proposal is not a full application design specification or a claim that the proposed component combination has been qualified.

## Recommendation

Build a small desktop shell around napari’s multidimensional image and annotation layers, with an application-owned project service and a deliberately narrow import contract. Use `tifffile` for a qualified subset of OME-TIFF, Zarr v2 for local OME-NGFF 0.4 stores, and Dask-backed lazy reads where the selected reader supports them. Keep annotations, interpretation choices, provenance, display state, and operation history in a transactional SQLite project database. Keep source image bytes immutable and outside SQLite. Store masks and derived image arrays as separate immutable chunked artifacts.

The initial support promise should be limited to local, single-file OME-TIFF and local OME-NGFF 0.4/Zarr v2 inputs with explicit, supported axes and readable chunks/tiles. Import one selected image series at a time. Reject or quarantine other layouts with a readable reason, preserve the input and extracted metadata, and let a researcher explicitly map an ambiguous axis or calibration before proceeding. A mapping is a project interpretation record, not a rewrite of the source metadata. Preserve a full application-owned spatial mapping because the NGFF 0.4 transform vocabulary documented here is not a general affine vocabulary.

This choice is evidence-informed but still a proposal. The napari and tifffile pins are individually source-backed; their full cross-platform dependency and UI combination is not executed or qualified here. The 3 GB bounded-access objective is an engineering target, not an established performance result.

## Findings that affect the plan

**[External facts]** OME-NGFF 0.4 describes chunked Zarr v2 storage, axis metadata, multiscale arrays, channel display hints, and label images. Its axes carry names and optional types/units; axes and array dimensionality must agree. Its documented coordinate transformation types are identity, scale, and translation; multiscale level transformations are scale and optional translation. Therefore this version’s standard transform metadata cannot by itself encode an arbitrary rotation or shear. Its image-label convention is integer label arrays linked to an image; it is not a general vector-annotation or project-provenance format. (S1 §§2, 3.1, 3.3–3.7.)

OME-TIFF maps image planes to TIFF IFDs using OME-XML `TiffData` and dimension-order information. A reader must interpret that mapping rather than assume that raw page order is the application’s canonical axis order. (S2, “DimensionOrder” and “The TiffData element”.) The pinned tifffile release documents OME-TIFF support and an `aszarr=True` route for region access with Zarr 2; it also documents compatibility limits, including no support for Zarr 3 in that release and an OME-TIFF limitation for multi-file pyramidal data. This supports a narrow reader experiment, not universal format support or a performance guarantee. (S3, release 2025.5.10 description.)

Napari v0.6.5 source shows lazy wrapping of Zarr data for slicing and transform-aware channel splitting. A real defect report says splitting RGB could lose affine metadata. The linked fix changes the split path to preserve layer metadata, and its regression test checks scale, translation, and an affine case. The code and test appear in the v0.6.5 tag. This is a concrete warning to test metadata through every view/layer operation. The regression does not establish project persistence, export correctness, all rotation/shear paths, or whole-application behavior. (S4–S7.) Separately, an open napari issue reports slow/over-fetching behavior for a particular n-dimensional multiscale Zarr slicing path. It is an issue report, not a verified universal defect or a repair history; it reinforces that “chunked” and “lazy” must be measured with the actual slicer and reader. (S9.)

SQLite documents atomic transactions and rollback-journal recovery subject to assumptions about locking, writes, and flush behavior. This is a basis for a local project database with staged artifact writes, not a guarantee that arbitrary shared filesystems provide those assumptions. (S10.)

**[Engineering inferences]** Keep a single application-level axis map and spatial transform as the authority; treat reader metadata, napari layer transforms, display coordinates, and export conventions as adapters around it. Never let a view operation silently become a data transform. Store separate original metadata and user interpretation so a correction remains auditable. Put large pixel arrays outside SQLite and bind them to a project revision only after they are completely written and verified. A chunked format alone does not demonstrate bounded resident memory, cancellation, or acceptable latency.

**[Product choices]** The MVP should prefer explicit refusal and recoverability over silently guessing axes, units, or file layouts. There is one writer per project. Exchange is by closed bundles; simultaneous edits and automatic merging are deferred. Display changes are reversible view state and do not change samples. Geometry operations create a new derived data object with provenance. The source image is never modified in place.

## Supported input boundary and failure behavior

**[Product choice] Initial qualification candidate:**

- Local OME-TIFF in one file, with one researcher-selected image series, supported pixel types and installed/tested codecs. Start with 2D or explicit `ZYX` spatial data and optional explicit time/channel axes. Treat non-planar, packed-color, pyramidal, and multi-file cases as out of scope until separately qualified.
- Local OME-NGFF 0.4 using Zarr v2, with a 2–5 dimensional image whose axes have unique names and can be explicitly interpreted as the supported time, channel, and spatial axes. Start without HCS/plate, mosaic, arbitrary custom axes, or cross-array registration. Validate level shapes, transforms, units, and chunk layout before enabling the image.
- Integer and floating samples only where the selected codec/read path and display mapping have been tested. The first qualification matrix should include `uint8`, `uint16`, `int16`, and `float32`; support for additional dtypes is a later explicit choice. Do not coerce source values at import.
- A chunk/tile layout with individual read units that fit the selected in-flight memory limit. Inputs with giant strips/tiles that defeat the budget are rejected or opened in an explicitly degraded, cancelable mode only after measurement.

This is a proposed boundary, not a claim that every listed combination already works. Resolve whether pyramidal single-file OME-TIFF, BigTIFF, unusual endianness, each codec, and 64-bit floats belong in the first release by tests against exact fixtures. tifffile’s documentation itself describes limitations, so do not advertise “TIFF support” without qualification. Unsupported proprietary formats can be considered later through a separately qualified adapter; never overwrite the original during conversion.

On import, show the detected file/group, series, shape, axis names/order, sample type, units, channel metadata, calibration, transform interpretation, tiling/chunk sizes, and warnings. Retain original metadata bytes or an exact extracted metadata snapshot and a digest. If axes or calibration are missing, conflicting, or unsupported, offer only these clear choices: correct/map them explicitly and save that interpretation with the project; proceed in pixel coordinates with physical coordinates marked unavailable; or cancel. Never silently infer micrometres, orientation, a Z dimension, or a channel meaning. If the input cannot be decoded or an override cannot be validated, leave it untouched, retain the project/import report, and make no partially trusted image available for annotation.

## Proposed component layout and pins

**[Product choice]** Use a thin Qt desktop shell and napari v0.6.5 layers for image display, points, shapes, and labels. Keep document state, coordinate conversions, source identity, save/recovery, and export in project-owned services. Napari is a viewer/layer component, not the project format or the authority for scientific coordinates. Use `tifffile==2025.5.10` for the candidate OME-TIFF reader and `zarr==2.18.7` with a locked Dask array dependency for the candidate lazy access path. Napari v0.6.5’s captured package metadata requires Dask arrays and tifffile and declares Zarr 2 as an optional base; the tifffile 2025.5.10 description specifies Zarr 2 compatibility. These source constraints make the pins plausible together, but do not prove that one resolved Qt, Python, OS, and codec matrix works. Produce a complete lockfile and qualify it before adoption. Record Python, napari, reader, Zarr, Dask, Qt binding, codec, and application versions in each project and export manifest. SQLite is the transactional project index, not an array store.

Suggested modules:

1. **Import adapters:** strict OME-TIFF and NGFF parsers/readers, with an explicit conversion from source axes to the project axis model. Keep raw metadata snapshots and parser warnings.
2. **Project service:** SQLite schema/migrations, asset identities, interpretations, display preferences, annotations, command history, and derivation records. One writer; all state changes through transactions.
3. **Array service:** read-only source handles, asynchronous slice/tile requests, bounded decoded cache, cancellation tokens, progress, and immutable derived chunk stores.
4. **Viewer adapter:** translates between named project axes and viewer dimensions/layers; turns pointer events into sample-coordinate annotations; receives display preferences without changing samples.
5. **Exporter:** writes temporary outputs, manifests/sidecars, and readback verification, then publishes completed outputs.

## Coordinate, image, and annotation contract

**[Product choice]** Every image record retains the source’s original axis names/order and shape. Each axis has a stable name, role (`space`, `time`, `channel`, or explicitly mapped custom role), optional unit, length, and source location. Axis order is never inferred from a label such as “stack”; array index tuples always travel with their named axis order. Time and channel indices are selection coordinates, not spatial coordinates.

Spatial samples use zero-based continuous index coordinates where each integer denotes the center of that sample. Store coordinates by named axes and provide a canonical spatial vector in declared order, usually `index_xyz = [x,y,z]`; never reverse array `zyx` implicitly or discard the source axis order. For `t,c,z,y,x`, the selected array sample tuple `[t,c,z,y,x]` maps to spatial `index_xyz=[x,y,z]` and distinct `t`/`c` selections. A 2D source has only X and Y unless a physical plane embedding is explicitly present; do not invent Z.

Store physical calibration as an explicit affine map in float64, with dimensions and coordinate-frame labels: `physical = origin + M × index`. The source schema must state the input and output coordinate-axis names, units, frame identifier, sample-center convention, and matrix order. A 3D map may be represented by a 3×3 linear matrix plus a 3-vector origin (or equivalent homogeneous 4×4); a 2D map uses its actual dimensionality. Permit sign flips, axis permutations, rotation, and shear when the matrix is valid. If calibration is absent or ambiguous, retain the raw value/text and record `physical_mapping = null` until the user supplies a documented mapping. Never fill missing numbers with a default spacing. Use enough precision to avoid rounding coordinates when reopening or exporting.

Canvas/screen pixels are view coordinates only. Zoom, pan, rotation, device pixel ratio, channel contrast, and slice projection belong to serialized viewer state; convert pointer locations back through the current view mapping before storing geometry. A display transform cannot alter the canonical sample index or physical affine. Record window/level per channel and its convention; for a center `L` and width `W>0`, a baseline display mapping clips samples at `L-W/2` and `L+W/2`, maps the interval to `[0,1]`, and applies the selected LUT/gamma only for display. Store the exact parameters and software convention. Changing them changes no source bytes or derived sample array.

Each annotation has a stable UUID, author/local identity, created/updated time, type, geometry dimensionality, axis-index geometry, source asset ID and content identity, scope, style ID/version, and optional note/tags. Scope is explicit: plane (with named spatial slice and time/channel context), volume, time point, or whole acquisition. A point or polygon on one plane stores its fixed slice/time coordinates separately from its within-plane vertices. A volume annotation stores 3D sample coordinates. A mask is a chunked integer label image on a declared grid with explicit label values and mapping; no implicit resampling. Styles are reusable named/versioned objects with marker, stroke/fill, color, and width, with data-space versus screen-space width stated. Use napari points/shapes/labels as editing views; the project database/array artifact is authoritative.

Undo/redo records annotation commands and reversals in the project service, grouped as user-visible editing transactions. It covers geometry, deletion, scope/style assignment, and mask edits. A mask edit writes a new immutable chunk/revision or journals a reversible patch; it never risks a half-mutated source array. Keyboard navigation must include previous/next slice, time, channel, zoom/pan, tool selection, undo/redo, save, and accessible shortcuts. Provide keyboard focus order, labeled controls, visible focus, non-color scope/state indicators, and legible metadata, progress, and error messages.

