## Project identity, save, recovery, and storage

**[Product choice]** Use a directory project containing `project.sqlite`, a metadata snapshot area, optional portable source copies, and immutable `derived/` artifacts. Keep the SQLite database small: project/asset records, metadata and interpretation references, user display state, annotation geometries, operation/undo records, and artifact manifests. Store masks and derived pixels in chunked files. Project revisions and artifact pointers are explicit.

A source asset ID is independent of its path. For a single file, calculate a streaming SHA-256 over the bytes. For a Zarr directory, define a versioned deterministic tree identity: sorted normalized relative paths, each object’s size and content hash, then a canonical manifest hash. Record the algorithm/version, identity status, byte sizes, and last verified timestamp. Hashing is cancelable and reports progress. Until complete, identity is `pending`; an interrupted hash cannot be presented as verified. A relocated external source is accepted only after a full identity match (or a researcher explicitly creates a new asset and records that decision). Never relink by filename and size alone. A mismatch preserves the project and annotations, marks the source unavailable/changed, and blocks “verified against original” exports.

Default import references the original path read-only to avoid an unrequested second copy of a multi-gigabyte file. An explicit “make portable copy” option copies bytes to a project staging path, hashes and verifies them, flushes them, and renames within the same filesystem before the database commits the new asset reference. References save disk space but require relocation/relink when a workstation path differs. Portable copies travel more reliably but need at least the source’s storage size plus project/derived space and copy time. Show which mode was chosen and storage required.

For metadata/annotation saves, use a single-writer SQLite connection and explicit transactions. Prefer rollback-journal mode and synchronous durability settings selected and documented by the application. Stage large arrays/artifacts outside the database: write to a temporary sibling directory, flush and verify all files, then publish by same-filesystem rename before committing the database pointer in a transaction. A crash before the database commit can leave an unreferenced staged/final artifact; recovery reports it and can garbage-collect only after checking references. A committed DB pointer must never point to a known partial artifact. On startup, recover SQLite journals, validate recent references, offer the last consistent revision, and preserve a recoverable copy before repair. For export, write to a sibling temporary file/folder, close and read back, then publish; on failure, retain the prior output and label the temporary as incomplete.

SQLite’s documented atomicity depends on operating-system and filesystem behavior, especially lock and flush guarantees. Therefore do not have several workstations concurrently open the same live database over shared storage. Each researcher edits a local copy; close it and verify it before copying a project bundle to ordinary shared storage. The MVP does not merge concurrent edits. Conflicting revisions must be copied to distinct names and reviewed manually. Test the actual shared-storage/filesystem behavior before relying on rename or power-loss guarantees. Back up the last good database and record a project revision before schema migration.

## Minimum workflow and exports

1. Create a project and select a local source. Choose linked read-only reference or explicit portable copy. Show detected metadata and provenance, axes, calibration, shape/dtype, chunk/codec support, warnings, and identity progress. Unsupported or ambiguous input remains untouched; correction, pixel-only import, or cancel is explicit and recorded.
2. Navigate images and choose channel visibility, window/level/LUT, slice and time point. Preserve display settings per asset. Visually distinguish original source, derived arrays, and annotation overlays. No display adjustment writes samples.
3. Add points, polygons, and masks with reusable styles. Make annotation scope and relevant time/slice/channel context visible. Support keyboard navigation and undo/redo. Every shape remains tied to a source identity and coordinate frame.
4. Save, close, reopen, and check source identity. On another machine, resolve a verified portable copy or explicitly relocate a reference by content hash. Keep annotations inspectable if the source is missing, but do not claim alignment or verified export without the matching source.
5. Export an annotation table, overlay, and a small derived image. Show a summary of exactly what is copied, transformed, rendered, or omitted. Read each export back and verify its declared geometry/grid and source reference before marking complete.

The annotation table should include feature ID/type/scope, source asset ID and digest, original axis names/order, named sample indices, geometry, time/slice/channel context, optional physical coordinates, units/frame, style/version, and provenance. Export polygon coordinates in a documented JSON geometry column or sidecar rather than flattening them to a lossy point list. For masks, include label values, axis/grid manifest and a relative mask artifact path. Keep floating precision sufficient for the saved geometry. CSV alone cannot reliably express a full project contract, so pair it with a versioned JSON manifest/sidecar.

A rendered overlay (for example PNG) is a display artifact tied to a specific asset identity, time/channel/slice, view transform, and grid. It preserves rendered pixels, not the original intensity samples, complete calibration, or editable geometry by itself. Export a sidecar carrying the grid mapping, source identity, scope, and annotation IDs. An overlay must not be presented as a calibrated original image without this context.

A small derived image export records the selected region/axes, level, crop, dtype, source identity, operation, and application/reader version. If it copies samples without resampling or intensity mapping, record that; if it rescales, projects, crops, resamples, casts, or bakes a LUT, record the precise operation and mark any loss. Use OME-TIFF or NGFF metadata where supported plus an application sidecar. Because NGFF 0.4 does not encode an arbitrary affine, either retain the exact full affine in the sidecar and warn that standard metadata alone is incomplete, or explicitly resample into a representable grid as a new derived-data operation. Never silently discard rotation/shear. The source remains byte-identical.

## Opportunities, alternative, assumptions, and validation gates

**Useful opportunity — [External fact + product choice]:** NGFF image-label groups provide a standard route for integer mask interchange linked to an image. Implement optional mask export after the core project format works. Keep points, polygons, undo history, arbitrary app affine, and provenance in the project/sidecar because the cited label convention does not supply those project semantics. (S1 §§3.6–3.7.)

**Plausible alternative — [Product choice]:** If an instrument’s important proprietary format cannot be supported by the strict readers, retain it as an immutable original and add a separately qualified Bio-Formats or vendor conversion adapter that creates a documented derived OME-Zarr copy. Compare that work with a simple user-directed conversion outside this app. Do not add a general plugin framework or claim support until exact fixture coverage, metadata mapping, and failure recovery are demonstrated. This is an alternative to widening the MVP parser surface, not a recommendation to convert every input.

**Critical assumptions to validate:**

- The exact napari 0.6.5, Qt, Python, tifffile 2025.5.10, Zarr 2.18.7, and Dask lock resolves on each workstation OS and remains maintainable. Captured metadata is not an environment build.
- The target microscopes produce inputs within the proposed OME-TIFF/NGFF subset, including chunk/tile sizes and codecs that support bounded reads. The research group should inventory a consented fixture corpus and include malformed and ambiguous examples.
- Pointer/event coordinates can be consistently transformed through viewer zoom/rotation and converted to the project’s sample-center convention, including channel splitting and dimension changes.
- Streaming whole-file or tree hashing is acceptable for workflow time. The project must not claim identity verification until hashing finishes.
- SQLite journaling, file locks, flushes and rename are reliable on the actual shared storage. Local staging and closed-bundle exchange are the initial safety boundary.
- The export ecosystem accepts sidecars for full affine and annotation context. If downstream tools ignore sidecars, the app must identify a standard-only export as lossy or offer an explicitly resampled derivative.

**[Proposed validation; all UNEXECUTED]** Qualify in stages before a release. The witness inventory has check IDs and precise expected observations.

1. Resolve and install the locked build on each OS; reopen a common project across machines. Record dependency versions and failures.
2. For representative 3 GB tiled OME-TIFF and chunked NGFF, compare windowed slice access with a reference read. Measure peak RSS, cache bytes, maximum in-flight chunks, disk traffic and p50/p95 warm/cold slice latency under 16 GB RAM. A proposed initial budget is ≤512 MiB decoded cache and ≤4 concurrent reads with per-chunk admission capped at 32 MiB; validate that source reader buffers do not exceed assumptions. Proposed UI targets are p95 warmed slice/edit ≤250 ms, progress update ≤250 ms, and cancellation acknowledgement ≤1 s. These are targets to test, not observed component performance. Repeat with a giant strip/tile and worst-case compression to validate rejection/degraded behavior.
3. Hash the source before and after import, annotation, display changes, save/reopen, and export. Require exact match for every non-derived source. Kill the process during portable copy, mask write, DB transaction, project close, and export; reopen and verify a prior complete revision, no DB reference to partial data, visible cleanup candidates, and preserved inputs.
4. Move a linked source to another path and confirm only matching content can relink. Change one byte and confirm mismatch blocks verified export while annotations/project remain available. Repeat for a Zarr object and tree manifest.
5. Test axis order permutations, missing units, negative direction, anisotropic spacing, nonzero origin, 2D, time/channel stacks, and rotation/shear. Compare points/polygons before and after close/reopen/export to an independently calculated affine reference. Explicitly test view pan/zoom, channel split, slice navigation, screen pixel ratio, and save/reload. Include an unsupported/ambiguous axis that must stop until mapping, pixel-only, or cancel is chosen.
6. Write CSV/JSON table, overlay, mask/label image, and small derived image. Reopen/read back each output; compare sample hashes when no pixel operation is declared, verify mask grid/labels and geometry coordinates, and check source unchanged. Test both a representable scale/translation and a nonstandard rotated/sheared affine; the latter must carry sidecar or require explicit resampling. Display the export summary to researchers and ask them to identify preserved versus lost fields.
7. Exercise annotation undo/redo, crash during mask edits, style versioning, keyboard-only operation, screen-reader names, focus order, and high-contrast/legible warnings with representative users.

**Deferred useful leads:** broader reader coverage based on actual instrument inventory; pyramid/plate/mosaic support; NGFF masks; staged Bio-Formats/vendor conversion; bundle revision comparison or later merge; live collaboration; cloud sync; GPU acceleration; automatic segmentation; registration; and richer provenance interchange. Keep these leads visible, but they are not MVP obligations. The open napari slicing issue should be revisited when choosing the exact viewer release and source layout rather than treated as evidence that the proposed access path meets the target.

